   "subject": "Clustering on PCA data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Fist, sorry if the answer is trivial. Proficient in Delpgi, but just started R today. I am really glad I found your Cardinal package.<div><br></div><div>So: my R code</div><div>- loads the Analyze file to data</div><div>- normalizes data to data2</div><div>- runs PCA on data2, say PC1-PC15, and puts them into data3</div><div>- I would like to run spatialKMeans on data3 and put them to data4 but get an error message:</div><div><div><br></div><div>&gt; data4 &lt;- spatialKMeans(data3, r=2, k=15, method=&quot;adaptive&quot;)</div><div>Error in (function (classes, fdef, mtable)  : </div><div>  unable to find an inherited method for function ‘spatialKMeans’ for signature ‘&quot;PCA&quot;’</div></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Can you help me?</div><div><br></div><div>Many thanks in advance, Vilmos</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "giZIK24kBgAJ",
   "from": "kbemis",
   "author": "6033d3c0e7d242fa",
   "date": "2017-03-17T01:24:40Z",
   "subject": "Re: Clustering on PCA data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi Vilmos,</div><div><br></div><div>You should run spatialKMeans or spatialShrunkenCentroids on &quot;data2&quot;, which is the actual dataset, not on &quot;data3&quot; which is simply the results of PCA.</div><div><br></div>If you really want to do spatial k-means on the PC scores, you could create a new image dataset that uses the PC scores as the data.<div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><font color=\"#660066\"><span style=\"color:#000\">data4 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">SImageSet</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data3$scores</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> coord</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">coord</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>data5 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> spatialKMeans</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">, </span><span style=\"color:#000\">r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> k</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">15</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></font></div></code></div><br>However, the clustering would not be any better than if you just did spatial k-means on &quot;data2&quot; in the first place.</div><div><br></div><div>Furthermore, if you do spatialShrunkenCentroids, a major goal of the method is to allow interpretation of the t-statistics for each m/z feature. The t-statistics would not be as meaningful if they corresponded to the PC&#39;s instead.</div><div><br></div><div>-Kylie</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
